<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burchard of Würzburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 753</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Burchard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burchard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> monk who joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>'s mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 742 Boniface consecrated him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the newly founded diocese of Würzburg. He built up the church in the region, translated the relics of the Irish martyr Kilian to his cathedral, and took part in the reforming synods of the Frankish church. He is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burchard-of-wurzburg.jpg" alt="Burchard of Würzburg" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Salver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BurckarduswholeBW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Burchard, in German Burkard or Burkhard, was a Benedictine monk, possibly of noble birth, who was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmesbury-abbey">Malmesbury Abbey</a>. After the death of his parents he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and, some time after 732, joined Boniface, who may have been a relative, in his missionary work. He lived for a while at the Abbey of St. Peter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>, founded by Boniface, and later went as a missionary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Boniface organised the bishoprics of Middle Germany he placed Burchard over that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>. As was usual at the time, the appointment was approved by Carloman, mayor of the palace, who granted the diocese a number of endowments. Burchard officiated as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at the consecration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibald">Willibald</a> of Eichstädt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a> confirmed the new see in 743.</p><p>Burchard took part in the first German council in 742 and in the general Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 747. The following year, as Boniface's envoy, he presented the council's results to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 750, with Fulrad of Saint-Denis, he brought Zachary the question of Pepin, whose answer was taken to justify the assumption of royal power by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 751 Burchard resigned his see in favour of Megingoz, a Benedictine monk of St. Peter's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>, and withdrew to a life of solitude. In 752 he dedicated the Abbey of St. Andrew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, later renamed St. Burchard's Abbey, and on 8 July 752 he brought the relics of Saint Kilian and his companions Saint Totnan and Saint Colman to Würzburg Cathedral, which was then dedicated to Kilian. He died in 753. His own relics were moved to the Abbey of St. Andrew in 986, and his feast day is 14 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burchard of Würzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The diocese was founded in 742 by Saint Boniface, with <b>Burkard</b> as its first bishop.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Homburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=743" aria-label="England on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4528 338 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Burchard of Würzburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4978 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4978 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=743">What was here in 743 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
